Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian gothic period drama; naturalistic faces, fabric, fog, and candlelight; one consistent live-action look for all cast and spirits.

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

Fog hangs thick over cobblestones. A brass nameplate reads SCROOGE AND MARLEY. Cold bites the air.

(SOUND: distant church bell, wheezing footsteps, foghorn wind)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A mean little fire. EBENEZER SCROOGE, elderly, sharp-nosed, thin blue lips, frosty white rime on brows and chin, red-rimmed eyes, dark coat buttoned tight, scratches figures in a ledger. Beyond a thin partition, BOB CRATCHIT, thin, threadbare, long white comforter, copies letters at a desk with one coal of fire.

(SOUND: pen scratch, coal settle, fog pressing at the panes)

The street door bursts open. FRED, young, ruddy, handsome, eyes sparkling, breath smoking, strides in glowing from the cold.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with Merry Christmas on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it. I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Bob involuntarily applauds, then snuffs his last spark in fright.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle! And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves warmly, greeting Bob at the door. Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers, hats off.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Are there no prisons?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it. If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Night thickens. Scrooge buttons his great-coat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob snuffs his candle, winds his long white comforter, and hurries out into the fog.

(SOUND: door slam, cold wind, Bob's quick footsteps fading)

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A gloomy yard. Fog and frost cling to a black old gateway. Scrooge gropes to his door, key in hand. The large knocker becomes MARLEY'S FACE—spectacles on ghostly forehead, livid, eyes wide and motionless.

(SOUND: key scrape, sudden hush, low spectral hum)

Scrooge stares. The knocker is iron again. He turns the key sturdily, lights a candle, and bangs the door.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room. Small fire. Saucepan of gruel on the hob. Scrooge, in dressing-gown, slippers, and nightcap, double-locks the door and sits close to the meagre flame. Dutch tiles around the grate. Marley's face seems to swim on every one.

(SOUND: low fire crackle, house settling, distant wind)

SCROOGE
Humbug!

A disused bell begins to swing—softly, then loudly. Every bell in the house answers. They cease. A clanking rises from below, as of a heavy chain dragged over casks.

(SOUND: bells pealing together, then chain dragging up stairs)

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The heavy door opens of itself. MARLEY'S GHOST enters—pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots, transparent body, long chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, and steel purses clasped about his middle. The dying flame leaps up.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me? Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

The Ghost sits opposite the fire. Hair and tassels stir as by hot vapour.

SCROOGE
You don't believe in me.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. It removes the bandage from its head; the lower jaw drops upon its breast. Scrooge falls to his knees.

(SOUND: appalling chain rattle, jaw clack, Scrooge's gasp)

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will. Is its pattern strange to you? Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house—mark me!—in life. Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.

It flings the chain upon the ground.

(SOUND: heavy chain crash on floorboards)

MARLEY'S GHOST
At this time of the rolling year, I suffer most. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

SCROOGE
Couldn't I take 'em all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The Ghost binds its head again. The window rises. Wailings fill the air. Marley floats out into the bleak night. Outside, chained phantoms moan, unable to help the living. They fade into mist. Scrooge closes the window, tries to say Humbug, fails, and falls asleep upon the bed still dressed.

(SOUND: mournful spirit wails, wind through open sash, then silence)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Darkness. Church chimes strike twelve, then One—deep, dull, hollow, melancholy. Light flashes. Bed-curtains drawn aside by a hand.

(SOUND: deep ONE of the bell, rush of light)

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands close—like a child yet like an old man; white hair, unwrinkled face with tender bloom; long muscular arms; bare feet; pure white tunic trimmed with summer flowers; holly branch in hand; bright jet of light springing from its head; great extinguisher-cap under its arm. The figure flickers, now clear, now dissolving.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past. Your past. Rise! and walk with me!

It clasps his arm. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Clear cold winter day. Snow on the ground. Fields on either hand. A little market-town in the distance.

(SOUND: crisp snow underfoot, distant boys shouting Merry Christmas, pony hooves)

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us. The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

INT. BOARDING SCHOOL CLASSROOM - DAY

A long bare melancholy room. Deal forms and desks. YOUNG SCROOGE, a lonely boy, reads near a feeble fire. Adult Scrooge sits and weeps.

(SOUND: mice behind panelling, drip from a water-spout, soft fire click)

A little girl, LITTLE FAN, darts in and throws her arms about the boy's neck.

LITTLE FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. And you're to be a man! And first, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

YOUNG SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature. But she had a large heart! She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child. Your nephew!

Scrooge looks uneasy. The Ghost waves its hand.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

Old FEZZIWIG in a Welsh wig at a high desk. Clock points to seven. YOUNG MAN SCROOGE and DICK WILKINS hurry in.

(SOUND: lively fiddle tuning, laughter, shutters slamming up)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here!

The warehouse becomes a bright ball-room. Mrs. Fezziwig, three Miss Fezziwigs, apprentices, housemaid, cook, milkman—all pour in. Dancing, cake, cold roast, mince-pies. Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig lead Sir Roger de Coverley; his calves shine like moons. At eleven they shake every hand at the door with Merry Christmas.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOR - EVENING

YOUNG MAN SCROOGE, face beginning to harden with care and avarice, sits with BELLE, a fair young girl in mourning-dress, tears in her eyes.

(SOUND: soft fire, her quiet tears)

BELLE
It matters little. Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG MAN SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Our contract is an old one. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man. I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. Another shadow: Belle now a comely matron, laughing among many children; her husband arrives with Christmas toys. He mentions seeing Scrooge alone in his office while his partner lay dying.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me? Remove me! I cannot bear it!

He seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon the Spirit's head. Light still streams from under it. Scrooge reels to his own bed and sinks into sleep.

(SOUND: struggle, extinguisher forced down, heavy breathing into silence)

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

The bell strikes One. A blaze of ruddy light from the adjoining room. Scrooge shuffles to the door.

(SOUND: deep One, roar of a mighty fire)

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING-ROOM - NIGHT

Transformed: living green on walls, holly, mistletoe, ivy; a mighty blaze; a throne of turkeys, geese, pigs, sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, oysters, apples, oranges, bowls of punch. Upon it sits the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—a jolly Giant in simple green robe bordered with white fur, bare breast and feet, holly wreath with icicles on dark brown curls, glowing torch like Plenty's horn.

(SOUND: fire roaring, punch seething, rich holiday murmur)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never. Spirit, conduct me where you will. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING - DAY

Snow on roofs. People scrape pavements, fling snowballs, hurry to church in best clothes. The Spirit sprinkles incense from his torch on poor dinners carried to bakers' shops; angry words turn to good humour.

(SOUND: church bells, snow shovels, cheerful street calls, torch hiss)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most. Because it needs it most.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT, brave in ribbons on a twice-turned gown, lays the cloth with BELINDA. PETER plunges a fork into potatoes, collar choking him. Two smaller Cratchits dance in smelling the goose. MARTHA arrives from work. BOB CRATCHIT enters with at least three feet of comforter, TINY TIM upon his shoulder—little crutch, iron frame on his limbs.

(SOUND: potato saucepan bubbling, crutch on floorboards, family chatter, pudding cloth steam)

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

They fetch the goose. Grace. Mrs. Cratchit plunges the carving-knife; stuffing gushes; Tiny Tim beats the table and cries Hurrah. The pudding arrives like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing in brandy, holly on top. Later, chestnuts sputter; Bob serves hot mixture from a jug in two tumblers and a custard-cup without a handle.

(SOUND: knife in goose, delighted murmur, brandy whoosh, chestnuts cracking)

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Bob holds Tim's withered little hand. Scrooge watches, moved.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head at his own words.

BOB CRATCHIT
I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year! He'll be very merry and very happy, I have no doubt!

The shadow of Scrooge's name darkens them a moment, then lifts. They fade in the Spirit's torchlight; Scrooge's eye stays on Tiny Tim.

EXT. BLEAK MOOR / LIGHTHOUSE / SHIP - NIGHT

Quick visions: miners' hut with old song; lighthouse men with grog wishing Merry Christmas; ship's crew humming Christmas tunes at the wheel. Then—

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - NIGHT

Bright, dry, gleaming. FRED laughs holding his sides. Niece and friends roar with him. Games of blind-man's buff and Yes and No. They toast Uncle Scrooge with mulled wine though he refused dinner.

(SOUND: hearty laughter, harp air, fire crackle, glass clink)

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! He wouldn't take it from me, but may he have it, nevertheless. Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge, invisible, would have pledged them if he could. The Ghost grows older; hair greys. Chimes ring three quarters past eleven.

INT. OPEN PLACE - NIGHT

From the Spirit's robe cling two children—wretched, ragged, wolfish: a boy and a girl.

(SOUND: midnight wind rising, children's shallow breath)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Giant is gone. A solemn Phantom draped and hooded comes like a mist along the ground—the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME. Only one outstretched hand is visible.

(SOUND: twelve deep strokes, then awful silence)

INT. CITY EXCHANGE - DAY

Merchants chink money. The Spirit points. Scrooge listens to a fat man and others.

(SOUND: coin chink, casual laughter, pocket watches)

FAT MAN
I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead. Last night, I believe. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral; for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

Another pair: "Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?" Cold seasonable talk. They stroll away. No one mourns.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BONE SHOP - NIGHT

Low-browed shop of iron, rags, bones. OLD JOE smokes by a charcoal stove. CHARWOMAN, MRS. DILBER the laundress, and UNDERTAKER'S MAN laugh over bundles plundered from a dead man—seal, pencil-case, sheets, sugar-tongs, bed-curtains, blankets, and the shirt from the corpse itself.

(SOUND: rusty hinge skreak, coins counted on ground, coarse laughter)

CHARWOMAN
Ha, ha! This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DARK BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Bare uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet, a body unwatched, unwept, uncared for. Pale light on the cover. A cat tears at the door; rats gnaw beneath the hearth.

(SOUND: cat scratch, rats gnawing, dead silence over the bed)

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. Let us go! If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. DEBTORS' HOME - DAY

A mother, CAROLINE, and children. Her husband enters with ashamed relief.

(SOUND: children's hushed play, clock tick)

HUSBAND
Bad. He is past relenting. He is dead. We may sleep to-night with light hearts, Caroline!

Their hearts are lighter for the death. Scrooge recoils.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - NIGHT

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and daughters sew. Peter reads. The noisy little Cratchits are still as statues. Bob enters, tries cheer, then breaks.

(SOUND: soft sewing, then Bob's stifled sob)

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

He goes upstairs, kisses Tiny Tim's face in the Christmas-hung room, returns composed. They promise never to forget Tim's patience. Bob is very happy in their love. Scrooge turns to the Spirit.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead!

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

Walled in, overrun by grass and weeds. The Spirit points to a neglected grave. Scrooge creeps toward it trembling.

(SOUND: wind in weeds, distant toll, Scrooge's shaking breath)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only? Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Spirit remains immovable. Scrooge reads the stone: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no! Spirit! hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope! Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life!

The kind hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He clutches the hand. The Phantom repulses him. The hood and dress shrink, collapse, and dwindle into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is his own. Soft daylight. Scrooge scrambles out of bed, weeping and laughing, face wet with tears.

(SOUND: lusty church peals—clash, clang, hammer, ding, dong—sweet and glorious)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! They are not torn down—rings and all. I am here—the shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled. I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He runs to the window, opens it. No fog. Clear, bright, jovial cold. Golden sunlight. A boy in Sunday clothes below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner? Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there?—Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck! Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot. Scrooge rubs his hands.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

He pats the knocker with love, pays for turkey and cab with endless chuckles, dresses in his best, and goes out.

EXT. LONDON STREETS - DAY

People pour forth. Scrooge smiles at all. Good-humoured fellows wish him merry Christmas; he answers with delight. He meets the Portly Gentleman.

(SOUND: bright street bustle, merry greetings, church bells continuing)

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. A merry Christmas to you, sir! Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

He whispers in the gentleman's ear.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you come and see me?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. Bless you!

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - DAY

Scrooge sidles his face round the door. Table spread in great array.

(SOUND: warm room hush, then explosion of welcome)

SCROOGE
Fred! It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred nearly shakes his arm off. Niece, Topper, plump sister—wonderful party, wonderful unanimity, wonderful happiness.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

Next morning. Scrooge arrives early, door wide open to the Tank. Clock strikes nine. A quarter past. Bob, full eighteen minutes and a half late, plunges to his stool and drives his pen.

(SOUND: clock strike, Bob's hurried pen, Scrooge's feigned growl)

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. It's only once a year, sir. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He leaps from his stool and digs Bob in the waistcoat.

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob trembles between ruler and joy.

EXT. LONDON - DAY (MONTAGE)

Scrooge becomes a second father to Tiny Tim, who does NOT die. He is as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knew. Some laugh at the alteration; his own heart laughs, and that is enough. Tiny Tim walks stronger beside him.

(SOUND: bright winter air, children's laughter, a final gentle bell)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle, ever afterwards; and it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed—

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.

THE END
